+//! Apple's extended-range forms over a buffer ([`Buffer::new_xr_texture`](crate::Buffer::new_xr_texture)):
+//! `BGR10_XR`, `BGRA10_XR`, the two-plane `RGB10A8_2P_XR10` and their `_sRGB` twins. Vulkan has
+//! no format of their bytes, so such a texture's texels live in an optimal `R16G16B16A16_SFLOAT`
+//! image of its own (the runtime's copies over buffers, `crate::over`), and where the plain
+//! texture over a buffer copies the bytes in and back, a compute shader (`xr_convert.comp`)
+//! decodes Apple's bytes into the image's values and encodes them back, so the CPU and every other
+//! process that reads or writes the buffer (an IOSurface's pages) sees Apple's bytes.
+//!
+//! The encodings, as `metal::format::ExtendedRange` records Metal's answers (measured on an Apple
+//! M3 Max and an M4, every code; Apple's `MTLPixelFormat` reference names the bit order):
+//!
+//! - A colour code `c` (10 bits) is the value `(c - 384) / 510`: code 384 is 0.0, 894 is 1.0, 0 is
+//!   -0.752941 and 1023 is 1.252941 (Apple's documentation quotes the range as up to 1.25098,
+//!   code 1022, the largest a render pass's tile holds). A write clamps the value to
+//!   `[-384 / 510, 639 / 510]`, a NaN as 0, and stores `roundEven(v * 510) + 384`.
+//! - Alpha (`BGRA10_XR`) reads its code clamped to `[384, 894]`, `[0, 1]`, and a write clamps the
+//!   value to `[0, 1]`. The two-plane form's alpha is an 8-bit unorm byte, `b / 255`, written
+//!   `roundEven(clamp(v, 0, 1) * 255)`. `BGR10_XR` reads alpha 1.
+//! - An `_sRGB` twin's colour value is in extended sRGB: a read takes it to linear by the sRGB
+//!   transfer of its magnitude, the sign kept, and a write the other way (Metal's own tables meet
+//!   the transfer within a code, `lower::arith::extended_range`). The image holds the linear
+//!   values, so shaders sample, blend and render linear values, as Metal's sRGB forms do. Alpha is
+//!   linear.
+//! - Bytes: `BGR10_XR` is one little-endian 32-bit word, blue in bits 0-9, green 10-19, red 20-29;
+//!   bits 30-31 are not read and are written as ones. `BGRA10_XR` is four little-endian 16-bit
+//!   fields, blue, green, red, alpha from the lowest, each code in a field's top 10 bits (bits
+//!   6-15), the low 6 bits not read and written as zeros. `RGB10A8_2P_XR10` is plane 0 of
+//!   `BGR10_XR` words and plane 1 of alpha bytes.
+//!
+//! Each value goes through `half` in the image: every code of each channel decodes and encodes
+//! back to itself (the unit tests, over all 1024 codes).
+
+use crate::counters::Gauge;
+use crate::device::{lock, Core};
